<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    21-App-Sandbox / examples/attacker-app / AndroidManifest.xml

    WHAT THIS IS
        The manifest of a deliberately nosy but completely ordinary third-party
        application. Its only job is to answer one question honestly:

            "What can an ORDINARY app, with no dangerous permissions, actually
             reach of ANOTHER app on this device?"

    WHY IT MATTERS
        `adb shell` runs as UID 2000 in the SELinux domain `shell`, with no
        seccomp filter, a non-empty capability bounding set, and supplementary
        groups no application holds. It is not an app. A file you read or a
        provider you queried from `adb shell` is NOT thereby proven reachable by
        an attacker.

        This APK is installed like any other app. It receives an ordinary UID in
        the 10000+ range, runs in the `untrusted_app` SELinux domain with its own
        MCS categories, gets `Seccomp: 2` and an empty capability set, and
        declares exactly one permission — INTERNET, which is protectionLevel
        "normal", granted silently at install on every Android version, and never
        shown to the user.

        So what it can do, a malicious app can do. That is the entire value of
        the file you are reading.

        See ../../README.md sections 3.14 and 5.8, and
        ../../labs/lab-03-attacker-app.md.

    NOTE ON THE PROJECT LAYOUT
        Sources sit directly in this directory rather than under
        src/main/java/..., and build.gradle points the Android sourceSets here.
        That keeps the proof-of-concept to four readable files. If you prefer the
        conventional layout, move AndroidManifest.xml to src/main/ and the .java
        files to src/main/java/com/attacker/sandboxprobe/ and delete the
        sourceSets block.

    RELATED
        ../../../24-IPC/examples/attacker-app/  — the same idea aimed at the IPC
        surface specifically (activities, services, receivers, providers,
        abstract sockets, localhost, confused deputy). This one aims at the
        SANDBOX: private data, shipped code, shared storage, the clipboard,
        /proc, and package visibility. Use both.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="com.attacker.sandboxprobe">

    <!--
        THE ONLY PERMISSION WE DECLARE.

        INTERNET is protectionLevel="normal": granted automatically at install,
        never shown to the user, held by essentially every app on every device.
        It exists here purely so the probe can demonstrate that it is present as
        GID 3003 in /proc/self/status — see section 0 of the output.

        Everything else this app does — reading another app's APK, querying
        exported providers, listing shared storage, reading the clipboard,
        enumerating /proc — requires NO permission whatsoever. That is the point.

        Two optional permissions are commented out below. Uncomment them ONLY
        deliberately, and say in the report which of them you used, because each
        one changes the attacker's cost and therefore the severity of anything it
        finds.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
        Storage. `dangerous` on API <= 32; replaced by READ_MEDIA_* on 33+. Each
        requires a user prompt. If a finding only reproduces with one of these
        granted, that prompt IS part of the finding and must be stated.
    -->
    <!-- <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
                          android:maxSdkVersion="32" /> -->
    <!-- <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_IMAGES" /> -->

    <!--
        =====================================================================
        THE <queries> ELEMENT — DO NOT DELETE THIS
        =====================================================================

        From targetSdk 30 (Android 11) Android applies *package visibility
        filtering*. An app can no longer see every installed package by default.
        The consequences for a probe are silent and brutal:

          * PackageManager.getPackageInfo(target, ...) throws
            NameNotFoundException — exactly as if the app were not installed.
          * getInstalledApplications() returns a short, filtered list.
          * resolveActivity() returns null for components that genuinely exist
            and are genuinely exported.
          * ContentResolver.query() throws
            IllegalArgumentException: Unknown URL for a provider that is there.

        None of those look like "filtered". They all look like "not there". A
        tester who forgets this block concludes the target is unreachable and
        writes "no exported components are reachable from a third-party app".
        That is a false negative produced by their own manifest, and it is
        common enough that section 0 of this probe checks for it FIRST and tells
        you plainly which explanation applies.

        Package visibility filtering is a PRIVACY control, not a security
        boundary. It never makes an exported component unreachable — a real
        attacker simply declares the target here, or holds QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES.

        Edit these entries for each engagement.
    -->
    <queries>
        <!-- 1. The target package, by name. The most reliable form. -->
        <package android:name="com.example.target" />

        <!-- Add every package in scope, INCLUDING any that shares a signing key
             or a sharedUserId with the target. A shared UID group is analysed as
             one unit: the weakest member defines the security of all of them. -->
        <!-- <package android:name="com.example.target.updater" /> -->

        <!-- 2. Providers must ALSO be named by AUTHORITY. Declaring the package
                alone is not enough to resolve a content:// URI.
                Harvest the real ones:
                  # host
                  apktool d -f target.apk -o work/ >/dev/null
                  grep -oE 'android:authorities="[^"]*"' work/AndroidManifest.xml -->
        <provider android:authorities="com.example.target.provider" />
        <provider android:authorities="com.example.target.fileprovider" />

        <!-- 3. Intent-shaped visibility, so we can see who handles what. -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <data android:scheme="https" />
        </intent>

        <!--
            4. Last resort. QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES makes the whole device visible.
               It is a restricted permission on Google Play (a declared, approved
               use case is required), which is why real malware usually prefers
               the targeted forms above. On a test device it is harmless and
               convenient — but say in the report which form you used, because
               "we needed QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES" changes the attacker's cost.

               Uncomment BOTH lines together.
        -->
        <!-- </queries>
        <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES" />
        <queries> -->
    </queries>

    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:label="Flashlight"
        android:icon="@android:drawable/ic_menu_compass"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Material.Light">

        <!--
            The probe. Exported with a LAUNCHER filter purely so you can tap it,
            or start it with:
                adb shell am start -n com.attacker.sandboxprobe/.ProbeActivity

            The probe must be in the FOREGROUND for the clipboard section to
            return anything: since Android 10 an app may only read the clipboard
            while it holds input focus or is the default IME. If you read the
            clipboard from a background component and get null, that is the
            platform working, not the target being safe.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".ProbeActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="Flashlight">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
